<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Containers for work givers, carrying the natural order they scan in. Work that makes something
  scans before work that tidies: construction, then growing, then cutting and mining, then
  hauling. Cutting is ahead of mining because felling is the shorter job and the wood is usually
  nearer.

  Growing took cutting's old 1 when it arrived (U47), and the other three shifted down a rank to
  keep every pair distinct - the scan order among the original four is unchanged, but a field
  waiting for its seed now beats a tree waiting for its axe, which is the point: a zone is sown
  inside the daylight window or it is not sown today.

  The rate numbers are the work-speed curve (design 17 §3b): rate = base + slope × level, in
  thousandths of the speed everything is tuned at. The integers are INVENTED: anchored on OUR
  own starting roll (mean level 1.16) rather than the reference's level 8, with the reference's
  relative character kept — mining steepest, construction shallowest, hauling flat, because a
  skill drives either rate or quality and hauling has neither. Growing took cutting's numbers when
  it got its curve (2026-09-19): they are the two plant work types, so a difference between them
  would be a claim needing a measurement rather than the default.
-->
<Defs>

  <!-- No rateSkill: hauling has no speed stat at all, here and in the reference. -->
  <WorkTypeDef>
    <defName>Work_Haul</defName>
    <label>hauling</label>
    <order>4</order>
  </WorkTypeDef>

  <WorkTypeDef>
    <defName>Work_Cutting</defName>
    <label>cutting</label>
    <order>2</order>
    <rateSkill>1</rateSkill>
    <workRateBasePerMille>600</workRateBasePerMille>
    <workRateSlopePerLevel>100</workRateSlopePerLevel>
  </WorkTypeDef>

  <WorkTypeDef>
    <defName>Work_Mining</defName>
    <label>mining</label>
    <order>3</order>
    <rateSkill>2</rateSkill>
    <workRateBasePerMille>550</workRateBasePerMille>
    <workRateSlopePerLevel>105</workRateSlopePerLevel>
  </WorkTypeDef>

  <!--
    Construction scans before all three, which is the reference's own order and the right
    one here for a plainer reason: a site is work already begun, and a colony that wanders
    off to fell another tree with half a wall standing is a colony that finishes nothing.
    It costs the other three nothing while there are no sites, because both construction
    givers then answer in a single comparison.

    It covers carrying material to a site as well as working at one. Delivery is not a
    haul that happens to help: a haul takes a thing to a stockpile because it is tidier
    there, and this takes a thing to a site because a wall cannot be built without it.

    The two success numbers are the completion roll (U26): 850 in a thousand at level 0,
    50 more a level, certain from level 3. A novice botches about one wall in seven and
    loses the work and half the material; a builder the colony has grown never botches.
    Anchored on our own roll the way the reference anchors on its own — its certainty is
    at level 8 because that is where its colonists sit, ours is at 3 for the same reason.

    The rate numbers are the shallowest of the three curves on purpose: here skill buys
    quality and a lower failure rate as well as throughput, where mining buys nothing
    but speed.
  -->
  <WorkTypeDef>
    <defName>Work_Construction</defName>
    <label>construction</label>
    <order>0</order>
    <successBasePerMille>850</successBasePerMille>
    <successSlopePerLevel>50</successSlopePerLevel>
    <rateSkill>3</rateSkill>
    <workRateBasePerMille>700</workRateBasePerMille>
    <workRateSlopePerLevel>75</workRateSlopePerLevel>
  </WorkTypeDef>

  <!--
    Sowing and harvest are one work type (docs/design/22-growing.md section 6): one craft at one
    patch of soil, and a colonist who would sow but not reap strands the field at its only
    interesting moment. It scans behind construction and ahead of cutting - a site is work
    already begun, and a zone's daylight window is the one clock in the colony that will not
    wait, where a tree keeps until Thursday.

    And the skill finally buys speed at the hoe (owner, 2026-09-19: "make sure the speed of the
    sowing and harvesting is determined by the relevant skill"). The seam is this def and nothing
    else - WorkRatePerMille has carried the curve for every work type since WS2, and growing was
    the one def without a rateSkill. Cutting's own numbers, read as a judgement: a novice runs
    at six tenths of the tuned speed and each level buys a tenth more, so the tuned speed sits
    around level four where the starting rolls put a colonist who has hoed a season. The skill
    curve itself (17-rates-and-stats 3a) belongs to the skills work when it lands.
  -->
  <WorkTypeDef>
    <defName>Work_Growing</defName>
    <label>growing</label>
    <order>1</order>
    <rateSkill>4</rateSkill>
    <workRateBasePerMille>600</workRateBasePerMille>
    <workRateSlopePerLevel>100</workRateSlopePerLevel>
  </WorkTypeDef>

</Defs>
